WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.347
Four questions before you build an agent.

2
00:00:02.627 --> 00:00:13.635
Anthropic’s post states the principle bluntly: find the simplest solution possible, and only increase complexity when needed, which may mean not building an agentic system at all.

3
00:00:13.915 --> 00:00:21.445
Agentic systems trade latency and cost for better task performance, so the question is always whether the trade is worth it.

4
00:00:21.725 --> 00:00:26.355
Often a single call, optimized with retrieval and good examples, is enough.

5
00:00:26.635 --> 00:00:30.411
To make that judgment repeatable, this course uses four questions.

6
00:00:30.691 --> 00:00:41.997
They follow the checklist Barry Zhang, a co-author of the post, gives in his AI Engineer Summit 2025 talk (linked in the sources), phrased for a team decision.

7
00:00:42.277 --> 00:00:43.216
Complexity.

8
00:00:43.496 --> 00:00:47.080
Is the task multi-step and hard to specify in advance?

9
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:54.059
If you can draw the full decision tree, build the tree as a workflow: it is cheaper and you control every branch.

10
00:00:54.339 --> 00:00:58.328
“Turn this design doc into a working pull request” is open-ended.

11
00:00:58.608 --> 00:01:01.531
“Extract the decisions from this transcript” is not.

12
00:01:01.811 --> 00:01:02.621
Value.

13
00:01:02.901 --> 00:01:06.357
Does the outcome justify the extra cost and latency?

14
00:01:06.637 --> 00:01:09.645
Agents explore, and exploration burns tokens.

15
00:01:09.925 --> 00:01:14.085
A task worth a few cents per run can’t support a loop that costs dollars.

16
00:01:14.365 --> 00:01:15.304
Viability.

17
00:01:15.584 --> 00:01:19.211
Can current models actually do each critical part of the task?

18
00:01:19.491 --> 00:01:22.115
Test the weakest link before you build around it.

19
00:01:22.395 --> 00:01:26.043
If one capability is shaky, narrow the scope until it isn’t.

20
00:01:26.323 --> 00:01:27.304
Cost of error.

21
00:01:27.584 --> 00:01:31.467
When the agent is wrong, how bad is it, and how quickly will you find out?

22
00:01:31.747 --> 00:01:38.616
Errors that are cheap and easy to detect (a failing test, a draft a person reviews) make autonomy tolerable.

23
00:01:38.896 --> 00:01:47.003
Errors that are costly or silent (a wrong refund, an email to a client) demand approvals, read-only access, or no agent.

24
00:01:47.283 --> 00:01:49.864
If any answer is no, stay at a simpler tier.

25
00:01:50.144 --> 00:02:00.939
Coding agents are the standard example of a task that passes all four: the work is open-ended, valuable, within current models’ reach, and verifiable by tests and review.

26
00:02:01.219 --> 00:02:06.787
That last property is the one teams most often skip, and lesson 3 is about why it matters.
